evant to winds. Low value.\n  - **Play B1**: Isolated bamboo, no support in hand. Likely safe.\n\nBest improvement path: Minimal chance to improve. Possibly aim for **All Chows** (2 points) + **No Honors** (1 point) or **One Voided Suit** (1 point), but F1 breaks No Honors. Removing F1 helps avoid blocking future No Honors if possible.\n\nHowever, I already have a pung ([W3\u00d73]) and a chow ([B7,B8,B9]), so **Melded Hand** (6 points) is possible only if the final pair comes from another player. But currently, I don\u2019t have a pair.\n\nWait \u2014 do I have a pair?\nHand: [W2, W2] \u2192 yes! I have a **pair of W2**.\n\nSo current structure:\n- Meld 1: [B7,B8,B9] (Chow)\n- Meld 2: [W3,W3,W3] (Pung)\n- Pair: [W2,W2]\n- Remaining: W4, B1, F1 \u2192 need to discard one.\n\nTo win, I need one more meld (either chow or pung). With W4, B1, F1 all singletons, I must draw or claim to complete a third meld.\n\nBut again, **no way to complete a chow with W2**, because W3 is gone. So W2\u00d72 is just a pair, not expandable.\n\nNow, consider safety and efficiency:\n- Keep W2\u00d72 as pair.\n- Among W4, B1, F1 \u2014 which is least useful?\n\nF1: Not my wind, not prevalent wind (F2 = South), so Prevalent Wind and Seat Wind patterns don\u2019t apply. Useless.\n\nW4: Might help someone make W4-W5-W6? Possible, but W5 and W6 are rare (only 1 each unshown), so low risk.\n\nB1: Terminal, might help someone finish lower end. But T1 and W1 are still common.\n\nStill, **F1 is the most useless tile in my hand** \u2014 it doesn't help me, and while all honors are somewhat safe, it blocks \u201cNo Honors\u201d pattern and adds no value.\n\nBut wait: Do I want \u201cNo Honors\u201d? Only if I can go for **All Chows** (2 points) + **All Simples** (2 points)? But W3 is a terminal (Character-3? No \u2014 W3 is number 3, which is **not terminal**; terminals are 1 and 9). So W3 is simple. But B7,B8,B9 includes B9 \u2014 a **terminal**. So that chow contains a terminal \u2192 breaks **All 